// opencode.jsonc — global opencode configuration, managed by home-manager.
//
// Edit HERE (devrc/scripts/opencode/opencode.jsonc) then `home-manager switch`
// (or scripts/ship.sh). ~/.config/opencode/opencode.jsonc is a READ-ONLY store
// symlink — editing it directly is impossible, and editing it "successfully"
// would mean the symlink is gone and the file has drifted.
//
// ==========================================================================
// 🔴 TWO LAYERS, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS A SAFETY CONTROL.
// ==========================================================================
//
//   LAYER 1 — this file's `permission.bash` globs: FRICTION. Broad `ask` on
//     mutation-ish families so a human sees the command. Cheap, readable, and
//     STRUCTURALLY UNABLE to be airtight (see below).
//
//   LAYER 2 — ~/.config/opencode/plugin/guard.js -> guard_core.py: ENFORCEMENT.
//     It PARSES: splits on `;`/`&&`/`||`/`|`/`&`, strips `VAR=…` prefixes and
//     `sudo`/`doas`/`env`/`timeout`/… wrappers, recurses into `bash -c '…'`,
//     and reasons about argv. It throws from `tool.execute.before`, which hard-
//     blocks the call. This is what actually stands between an agent and an
//     irreversible action.
//
// WHY THE SPLIT. Globs match a command node's FULL TEXT, so "this command wipes
// a node" is not expressible — the set of spellings is unbounded. Two rounds of
// pattern-patching each closed the spellings we thought of and left the ones we
// did not. MEASURED at c1e4c02, all resolving ALLOW on the primary agent:
//     talosctl -n 192.168.50.94 reset          (a node wipe, no prompt)
//     talosctl --nodes=192.168.50.94 reset
//     rm -f -r /        rm --recursive --force /
//     mke2fs /dev/sdc   mkswap /dev/sdd
// because `"*talosctl reset*"` requires the tool and the verb to be ADJACENT,
// and `"*rm -rf*"`/`"*mkfs*"` only knew one flag order and one binary name.
// The DENY block had been given infix wildcards; the ASK block had not.
//
// 🔴 SO: DO NOT ADD A NEW DANGEROUS FAMILY HERE AND CONSIDER IT HANDLED. A glob
// added here buys a prompt for the spelling you thought of. If the action is
// irreversible, add a check to guard_core.py's "opencode" policy — that is the
// layer with a parser and the layer with a test matrix over nine prefixes.
//
// WHAT THE DENY BLOCK BELOW IS FOR (honestly): it is a short, deliberately
// NON-EXHAUSTIVE mirror of the guard's families in their naive spellings. It
// exists ONLY so that a failure to load guard.js does not silently remove every
// deny at once. It is defence in depth, not the control — every one of these
// patterns has known bypasses, which is the whole reason layer 2 exists.
//
// ==========================================================================
// Mechanics of layer 1 (measured on v1.18.18 — do not re-derive)
// ==========================================================================
//
// 🔴 PERMISSION ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS THE INVERSE OF CLAUDE CODE.
// opencode evaluates permission globs with LAST-MATCH-WINS over a FLAT ORDERED
// ARRAY (`opencode debug agent <name>` prints that array — it is the authority,
// not this file). So `"*": "allow"` must come FIRST and every deny/ask AFTER
// it, and THE BLOCK IS ORDERED: `"*": "allow"`, then every ASK, then every
// DENY. Deny-last is what makes a narrow deny beat a broad ask. Do not
// interleave them. If someone "tidies" these keys alphabetically, `"*"` (0x2A)
// still sorts to the front — so the obvious first-key assertion does NOT catch
// it — but the specific patterns reorder among themselves. scripts/tests/
// test_opencode_config.py pins the EFFECTIVE RESOLVED OUTCOME for exactly this
// reason; a key-order assertion alone is vacuous.
//
// 🔴 EVERY PATTERN IS LEADING-`*` AND INFIX-`*`. opencode matches a command
// node's FULL TEXT, so a bare `"git stash*"` is bypassed by any prefix
// (`FOO=1 git stash`, `sudo -n git stash`, `git -C /tmp stash` all MISSED it),
// and a `"kubectl delete*"` is bypassed by any INTERLEAVED FLAG
// (`kubectl -n foo delete pod`). Both bypassing forms are the house style
// (`KUBECONFIG=$KC_HOMELAB kubectl -n ns …`, `git -C <path> …`). `*` crosses
// spaces, `/` and `-`, so `*kubectl*delete*` catches every ordering. The
// deliberate cost is over-matching — `git log | grep push` prompts. An `ask`
// that fires too often on a mutation family is a far better failure than an
// `ask` that never fires on the spelling an operator actually types.
//
// Other measured facts baked into this file (v1.18.18, EXCEPT where a bullet
// says otherwise — the 2026-08-19 re-derivation could not reach three of them,
// and a claim relabelled to a version nothing measured it on is worse than a
// stale one, because it looks fresh):
//   * `ask` SEMANTICS (NOT re-derived at the pin — last measured on v1.18.16;
//     observing it needs a run that EXECUTES, which the engine tests refuse to
//     do): `opencode run` AUTO-REJECTS an `ask` (it prints "auto-rejecting");
//     only the interactive TUI turns one into a prompt. `opencode debug agent
//     --tool` AUTO-APPROVES it. So `ask` means "friction for a human", never
//     "a control on an unattended agent".
//   * only a `"*": "deny"` removes a tool from the request schema; a narrow
//     deny does not, and `"ask"` costs exactly what `"allow"` costs.
//   * `small_model` SCOPE (NOT re-derived at the pin, v1.18.16): it drives
//     TITLE GENERATION ONLY — not compaction. The real cheap-model lever is
//     pinning the hidden title/summary/compaction agents in the `agent` block
//     below.
//   * a tool listed in this global `permission` block is re-enabled on the
//     HIDDEN title/summary/compaction agents too, whose stock tool set is
//     EMPTY. Hence the explicit `"*": "deny"` on each of them below.
//   * DEPRECATED, deliberately absent — a config-SCHEMA claim about keys this
//     file does NOT set, which the resolved `debug agent` dumps structurally
//     cannot see, so it was carried forward at v1.18.16 rather than relabelled:
//     agent-level `tools`, `mode` (at config level), `layout`, `autoshare`,
//     `reference` (now `references`), `maxSteps` (now `steps`).
//     `theme`/`keybinds`/`tui` moved to a separate ~/.config/opencode/tui.json
//     and are NOT set here.
//   * there is NO `list` tool and NO `websearch` tool on 1.18.18. The real set is
//     exactly {bash, edit, glob, grep, invalid, question, read, skill, task,
//     todowrite, webfetch, write}. Naming a nonexistent tool here is a no-op.
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",

  "model": "openrouter/xiaomi/mimo-v2.5",
  // Title generation only. Compaction is pinned via the `agent` block.
  "small_model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",

  "autoupdate": false,
  "share": "disabled",
  "snapshot": true,
  "lsp": true,
  "formatter": true,
  "subagent_depth": 2,

  "compaction": {
    "auto": true,
    "prune": true,
    "reserved": 16000
  },

  // Cap a single tool result so one `kubectl get -A -o yaml` cannot blow the
  // window and force an immediate compaction.
  "tool_output": {
    "max_lines": 800,
    "max_bytes": 24576
  },

  "watcher": {
    "ignore": [
      "node_modules/**",
      ".git/**",
      "result/**",
      ".direnv/**",
      "**/*.enc.yaml"
    ]
  },

  "agent": {
    // The three HIDDEN system agents. These run on every session (titles) or on
    // every context overflow (summary/compaction) and have no reasoning
    // requirement — pinning them to the flash model is the actual cost lever,
    // since `small_model` above only covers title generation.
    //
    // 🔴 `"permission": {"*": "deny"}` is NOT optional. Their STOCK tool set is
    // EMPTY (measured: `enabled=[]` against a bare config dir), but every entry
    // in the global `permission` block below is appended to THEIR resolved list
    // too — which re-enabled bash, write, edit, task and skill on all three.
    // compaction runs automatically on every context overflow, so that handed a
    // shell and a writer to the cheap model on a path nobody watches, and made
    // every title generation carry the ~3,730-token skill catalogue.
    "title": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },
    "summary": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },
    "compaction": {
      "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
      "permission": { "*": "deny" }
    },

    // Built-in `plan` is only nominally read-only — it can still write and
    // shell out. Make it GENUINELY read-only so "let's plan this" cannot
    // mutate the tree.
    //
    // `task` and `write` are here because the global block below OVERRODE the
    // built-ins: stock `plan` resolves `task 'general' deny` (it may not spawn
    // the general subagent, which HAS a shell), and the global `"task": "allow"`
    // landed after it and flipped it to allow. Restating them at agent level
    // puts them last again.
    "plan": {
      "permission": {
        "edit": "deny",
        "write": "deny",
        "bash": "deny",
        "task": { "general": "deny" }
      }
    }
  },

  "permission": {
    // NOTE: `read` is deliberately ABSENT. opencode ships a built-in
    // `read: {"*": allow, "*.env": ask, "*.env.*": ask, "*.env.example": allow}`
    // — a blanket `"read": "allow"` here lands AFTER those and silently defeats
    // the `.env` guard on every agent. The default already allows everything
    // except `.env`, so there is nothing to add.
    // `list` and `websearch` are absent because NO SUCH TOOLS EXIST on 1.18.18.
    "glob": "allow",
    "grep": "allow",
    "edit": "allow",
    "write": "allow",
    "webfetch": "allow",
    "todowrite": "allow",
    "task": "allow",
    "skill": "allow",

    // Ask before burning a long unproductive loop, and before reaching outside
    // the project directory.
    "doom_loop": "ask",
    "external_directory": "ask",

    "bash": {
      "*": "allow",

      // ================= ASK: friction on mutation families ================ //
      // Every pattern is leading-`*` AND infix-`*`, so no prefix (`VAR=…`,
      // `sudo`, `git -C <path>`) and no interleaved flag (`kubectl -n ns …`)
      // slips past. Over-matching is deliberate — see the header.
      //
      // 🔴 These are FRICTION, not enforcement, and `opencode run` auto-rejects
      // rather than prompting. Anything irreversible belongs in guard_core.py.

      // --- git ---
      "*git*push*": "ask",
      "*git*commit*": "ask",
      "*git*rebase*": "ask",
      "*git*checkout*": "ask",
      "*git*restore*": "ask",
      "*git*branch -D*": "ask",
      "*git*worktree remove*": "ask",
      "*gh*pr merge*": "ask",

      // --- kubernetes / gitops ---
      "*kubectl*delete*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*apply*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*patch*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*scale*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*drain*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*edit*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*replace*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*exec*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*cp*": "ask",
      "*kubectl*rollout undo*": "ask",
      // `get secret -o yaml` prints every value base64-encoded, i.e. plaintext.
      "*kubectl*secret*": "ask",
      "*helm*upgrade*": "ask",
      "*helm*uninstall*": "ask",
      "*helm*rollback*": "ask",
      "*flux*suspend*": "ask",
      "*flux*resume*": "ask",
      "*flux*delete*": "ask",

      // --- talos --- (the RESET verb is enforced by guard_core.py, which is
      // the only layer that catches `talosctl -n <ip> reset`. This broad ask is
      // friction for every other node-level verb.)
      "*talosctl*": "ask",

      // --- secrets to plaintext ---
      "*sops*-d*": "ask",
      "*sops*--decrypt*": "ask",
      "*sops*exec-env*": "ask",
      // 🔴 NO `*` BETWEEN "age" AND THE FLAG — it must stay `*age -d*`, not
      // `*age*-d*`. The inner `*` made this match any command text containing
      // "age" followed LATER by "-d", and "age" is a substring of package,
      // packages, image, message, storage and manage while "-d" covers -db-,
      // --dir, --debug and --dry-run. MEASURED 2026-08-21: `ls packages/…-db-…`
      // and `grep … packages/…-db/…` both resolved ASK, and since `opencode run`
      // auto-rejects an ask rather than prompting, two real dispatches were
      // killed mid-task by it. The leading `*` is still required and still
      // correct — it catches `/nix/store/…/bin/age -d` and `FOO=1 age -d`.
      // Pinned by three MUST_ALLOW rows in test_opencode_config.py.
      "*age -d*": "ask",
      "*age --decrypt*": "ask",

      // --- whole-system state ---
      "*nixos-rebuild*": "ask",
      "*home-manager*switch*": "ask",
      // 🔴 LOAD-BEARING — do not delete it as "already covered".
      // `"*age -d*"` DOES match some spellings of this command by pure
      // coincidence ("nix-collect-gar(BAGE) -d" ends in the literal "age -d"),
      // but ONLY those spelling the flag immediately after, so
      // literal `-d`. MEASURED: with this rule deleted, `nix-collect-garbage`
      // and `nix-collect-garbage --max-freed 1G` both resolve ALLOW at both
      // layers. Two things follow, and they point opposite ways: this rule is
      // the real gate for the no-`-d` spellings, AND `"*age -d*"` must not be
      // deleted on the reasoning that GC is separately gated for the rest.
      "*nix-collect-garbage*": "ask",
      "*nix*profile remove*": "ask",
      // The mutating systemctl verbs SEEN SO FAR — this list is NOT exhaustive
      // and must not be read as "the mutating verbs are handled". MEASURED:
      // `mask`, `unmask`, `kill`, `isolate`, `set-property` and `edit` all
      // resolve ALLOW today. Read-only, high-frequency verbs (`status`,
      // `list-timers`) are deliberately absent — gating those is pure prompt
      // fatigue, which trains the operator to click through the ones that
      // matter. Adding a verb here buys a prompt for that spelling only.
      "*systemctl*start*": "ask",
      "*systemctl*stop*": "ask",
      "*systemctl*restart*": "ask",
      "*systemctl*enable*": "ask",
      "*systemctl*disable*": "ask",
      "*systemctl*daemon-reload*": "ask",

      // --- privilege escalation: gate the WRAPPER itself ---
      // 🔴 ALL THREE `sudo` ROWS ARE LOAD-BEARING — none is a duplicate, and
      // each was found unpinned by a separate mutation sweep.
      //   `"*sudo *"`   needs the LITERAL "sudo " (space) -> misses `sudoedit`
      //   `"sudo*"`     is ANCHORED -> misses sudo in non-leading position
      //                 (`FOO=1 sudo …`, which IS the house style)
      //   `"*sudoedit*"` covers their INTERSECTION, which neither had:
      //                 MEASURED ALLOW at both layers before this rule —
      //                 `FOO=1 sudoedit /etc/nixos/configuration.nix`,
      //                 `EDITOR=vim sudoedit …` (the NORMAL invocation, since
      //                 sudoedit needs $EDITOR) and
      //                 `/run/wrappers/bin/sudoedit …` (the NixOS path).
      // Unlike a flag spelling, `sudoedit` is a fixed BINARY NAME, so a single
      // leading-and-infix `*` genuinely closes the family rather than buying
      // one more spelling. Delete any row and the others do NOT cover it.
      "sudo*": "ask",
      "*sudo *": "ask",
      "*sudoedit*": "ask",
      "*doas *": "ask",

      // --- filesystem blast radius ---
      "*chmod -R*": "ask",
      "*chown -R*": "ask",
      "*rm*-r*": "ask",
      // `dd` stays ANCHORED / operand-scoped on purpose: a leading-`*` `"*dd *"`
      // also matches `git add src/` (…"add "…), and gating every `git add` is
      // exactly the prompt fatigue that trains an operator to click through.
      "dd *": "ask",
      "*dd if=*": "ask",
      "*dd of=*": "ask",

      // ================= DENY: naive-spelling backstop ONLY ================ //
      // 🔴 READ THE HEADER. These are NOT airtight and are NOT the control.
      // Every one has known bypasses (that is the measured history of this
      // file). They exist so that a guard.js that fails to load does not
      // silently remove every deny at once. The enforcement lives in
      // guard_core.py's "opencode" policy, which parses argv.
      "*git*add -A*": "deny",
      "*git*add --all*": "deny",
      "*git*add .": "deny",
      "*git*stash*": "deny",
      "*git*reset --hard*": "deny",
      "*git*clean -f*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf /*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf ~*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf $HOME*": "deny",
      "*talosctl*reset*": "deny",
      "*mkfs*": "deny",
      "*mke2fs*": "deny",
      "*mkswap*": "deny"
    }
  }
}
